WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.390
Let's talk about college and politics and
economics because I did a story a couple of

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:12.270
weeks ago about the future of the United States
in the context of so many of our systems seeming

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.269
completely unsustainable.

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:17.690
And when I did that story a couple of weeks
ago, we talked about inequality.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:22.170
We talked about the healthcare system which
causes countless medical bankruptcies every

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:26.960
year and we also talked about education where
we were seeing and we continue to see the

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.670
cost of college rising dramatically.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:34.350
Wages are relatively stagnant and this makes
college both less affordable and also less

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:39.360
of a good investment relative to the wages
that one would expect and that's what I want

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.090
to dig into today.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:47.760
I want to ask you, might we be getting to
the end of the college era as we may end up

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.790
calling it this era where of course you have
to go to college regardless of the cost to

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.360
have a shot in the economy.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.820
I'll explain.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:01.030
We've already talked about how the exploding
cost of college is far exceeding wage growth

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:06.140
and we are seeing more and more people saddled
with student debt without an ability to ever

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:13.119
pay it off because of the earnings of the
jobs that that college degree ultimately lands

00:01:13.119 --> 00:01:14.119
them in.

00:01:14.119 --> 00:01:19.920
And if college costs continue going up, it's
a foregone conclusion that fewer and fewer

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.070
people will be able to afford it.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:26.110
And by that I mean to be able to afford paying
off their student loans with the wages they

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:27.580
can expect to earn.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:33.729
I also said in our segment of last week or
the week before that in the interests of Maine

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:38.430
state, maintaining a profitable status quo
for the establishment and for corporations

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:44.170
and for political elites, every once in a
while a bone can be thrown at people to prevent

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.979
them from really protesting to change the
system.

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:52.360
And that this has done to keep the system
just barely sustainable.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:57.630
Passably sustainable so that the perpetual,
uh, status quo remains.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:03.710
And interestingly enough, the premium in terms
of net worth for college graduates over non-college

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:06.000
graduates is very quickly shrinking.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.890
Now I want to be clear here because this,
this is, this is important.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:15.959
There is a huge advantage in terms of income
for those with college degrees over those

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:17.850
without college degrees.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:22.000
But the advantage in net worth has been shrinking
over time.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.730
And part of the reason is that with stagnant
wages, with people who go to college, having

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:33.410
to pay off the student debt when the money
goes off to pay loans and student loan interest,

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:40.730
rather than building your net worth, it has
a vicious cycle where you earn higher wages

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:45.460
but more of the wages go to paying off student
loans because of the student loans.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:49.650
You end up having to charge a lot of your
day to day expenses to a credit card, which

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.629
means you're paying even more interest, which
further restricts your net worth.

00:02:53.629 --> 00:02:57.660
So you make significantly more money with
a college degree.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.500
But the premium in terms of the effect on
your net worth is becoming smaller and smaller.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:06.770
Now, in addition to this, we've also talked
about the political component to the whole

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:11.520
college space right now, which is that convenient
for the American, right?

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.750
They have been to some degree successfully
pushing the idea that college is merely liberal.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:22.580
Indoctrination colleges restrict conservative
speech and this has been priming a lot of

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.450
conservatives to already be skeptical of college.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:31.590
So we may actually see a movement away from
the requirement of a college degree for more

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:36.970
and more jobs that could reinforce a move
away from people going to college in the first

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:37.970
place.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:41.220
And in fact there are lots of companies, even
some financial companies where more and more

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.820
positions don't require a college degree.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:44.909
So what's going to happen?

00:03:44.909 --> 00:03:51.540
The left has to be careful because if as a
society, we move away from college education

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.180
as a requirement for jobs.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:59.090
If the economy can't support the student debt,
if college makes less sense over time because

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.920
of what it costs, that's one thing.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.480
But remember that Republican talking points
work way better on the uneducated.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:12.330
So if there is indeed a move away from college
education, it could be very, very bad for

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:13.470
the American left.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:19.329
Since we know that unbalance, the more educated
someone is, the more likely they are to vote

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:21.079
for a more progressive candidate.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:22.570
So I want to hear from you about this.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:23.570
Get in touch.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:26.410
What do you think will eventually happen with
the cost of college?

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.950
Are we potentially starting to see a move
away from the college era?

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:32.100
To call it something.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:35.960
Uh, and what do you predict as the political
future?

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.669
If there is this move away from college, let
me know.

00:04:38.669 --> 00:05:17.870
Including via voicemail, which is

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:29.440
where

00:05:29.440 --> 00:06:06.419
we

00:06:06.419 --> 00:06:29.009
are going next.

